[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known, with one fix queued. It holds three problems.
The desk merged them because the subject lines matched ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
and a subject line says what a person saw, not what broke.

## The split

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

Each problem now has its own ticket. The counts come from the export, after duplicate rows
are removed (the export repeats some reports; one id is one report).

## How to tell them apart

- **parser:** the tenant writes the day first, and the report came through the app, by email,
  or through booking-v2. The shared parser reads the date month first, so day and month swap:
  7 April becomes 4 July. This is the fix that is queued.
- **hint:** the tenant writes the day first and runs booking-v3, and the report came through
  the form. The form tells people to type month first, then reads what they typed day first.
  Day and month swap here too, but the queued parser fix does not touch the form.
- **one-day:** the date moves by one day and the month stays. "Tuesday 6 October showed as
  Monday." A parser that swaps day and month cannot do that. Two of these reports name
  different time zones for the same meeting, so look at time-zone handling first.

<Callout kind="warn">
Do not close the hint or one-day tickets when the parser fix ships. They need their own fixes.
</Callout>

## What intake tagged

Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser, not from the tenant. The table on #4471 shows
where the tag and the cause disagree.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## For the next big ticket

- Merge on cause, not on subject. The same subject came from all three problems.
- Read a sample of bodies before you mark a ticket known.

